WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.746
Planning starts with the workload and the RAM budget. Count more than the base image.

2
00:00:05.746 --> 00:00:21.202
Include the kernel, initramfs, root image, writable overlay, file cache, desktop environment, graphics stack, browsers, containers, development tools, package operations, and the applications the system actually runs.

3
00:00:21.202 --> 00:00:30.508
A design that looks fine immediately after boot may fail when a browser opens many tabs or when a package update writes thousands of files.

4
00:00:30.508 --> 00:00:40.057
Next, decide what must persist. User data is the obvious category, but it is not the only one. Logs may be needed for troubleshooting or audit.

5
00:00:40.057 --> 00:00:47.421
Configuration may need to survive across boots. Package databases and updates may need a maintenance path.

6
00:00:47.421 --> 00:01:00.449
SSH host keys, machine identity, VPN profiles, certificates, browser profiles, application caches, containers, virtual machine images, and local databases all need explicit decisions.

7
00:01:00.449 --> 00:01:09.108
If a value should survive, decide where and how. If it should not survive, document that too. Updates are often the hardest part.

8
00:01:09.108 --> 00:01:16.958
If the base system is read-only and changes land in a RAM overlay, ordinary package updates may disappear at reboot.

9
00:01:16.958 --> 00:01:22.946
That may be acceptable for a disposable live session, but not for a managed workstation.

10
00:01:22.946 --> 00:01:35.004
Possible strategies include rebuilding the base image, snapshotting a known-good state, maintaining a persistent writable layer, or applying updates in a maintenance boot.

11
00:01:35.004 --> 00:01:41.882
Each strategy has tradeoffs in complexity, rollback, and testing. Logs deserve special attention.

12
00:01:41.882 --> 00:01:49.084
RAM-only logs are useful for a clean reset, but poor for diagnosing a failure that happened before reboot.

13
00:01:49.084 --> 00:01:58.876
Persistent local logs can consume storage and reveal activity. Remote logs can preserve evidence without writing heavily to the local system.

14
00:01:58.876 --> 00:02:03.084
Choose intentionally. Measure under realistic conditions.

15
00:02:03.084 --> 00:02:13.199
Boot the system, run the expected workload, simulate updates, fill caches, restart services, and watch memory pressure. Test shutdown and reboot.

16
00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:21.453
Confirm what persists and what resets. Document RAM assumptions, persistent mounts, update steps, and rollback steps.

17
00:02:21.453 --> 00:02:25.338
RAM-backed systems reward planning and punish guesses.
